  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tackling the root causes of child labour in India spices supply chains</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Over 10 million children aged between 5 and 14 years old are engaged in labour in India, the majority of the children work in the agriculture sector. Child labour, especially in its most harmful forms, affects children&#x2019;s school attendance, health, physical and mental development, thus hindering their life chances. Recent assessments have raised concerns about the incidence of child labour in the spices sub-sector in India, notably in light of the seasonal needs for flexible workforce. The proposed action will contribute to building a strong understanding of and effectively tackling the underlying root causes of child labour in the spices sub-sector in India.Project A will enable to better comprehend the magnitude and modalities in which child labour might take place in Olam&#x2019;s supply chain of chili and cumin supply chain, and its impact on children&#x2019;s health, development and education. Project partners will also strive to apprehend the internal and external dynamics leading to or facilitating child labour in the spices sector, including potential combinations and/or interdependences between these factors.For project B, the four areas of work are currently being considered (to be confirmed after project A) including:1)	Improving access to safe spaces and support services for at-risk girls and boys; 2)	Working with caregivers, farmers and communities to address harmful socio-cultural norms; 3)	Providing farming families with opportunities for enhanced livelihoods, thus tackling the economic root causes of child labour; and 4)	Bringing about systemic changes in practices and regulations by working with authorities and sectoral actors to help protect children from harmful labour.The ultimate aim is to eliminate child labour from Olam&#x2019;s supply chain in these zones.</narrative>
  </description>
